          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000009",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b",
            "visible": true,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          }
        ],
        "content": {
          "attachments": {
            "fig-before-after-red.png": {
              "image/png": ""
            }
          },
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**Timure and Rasuwagadhi, the same six kilometres square, before the flood and after it.**\n\n![Timure and Rasuwagadhi, before and after](attachment:fig-before-after-red.png)\n\nA thread of water through forest becomes bare rock and gravel from one valley\nwall to the other. The settlement beside it is gone with the bank it stood on.\n\nPale grey is ground no satellite saw clearly in the days after. Every pass was\n70 to 90 per cent cloud, so both sides are built from the clear pixels of\nseveral passes.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%",
          "title": "The steep gorge, before and after"
        }
      }
    },
