            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000007",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000008",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000009",
            "visible": true,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          }
        ],
        "content": {
          "attachments": {
            "fig-reflight.png": {
              "image/png": ""
            }
          },
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**Every count of this flood is provisional, and they do not agree with each\nother.**\n\nFour organisations counted destroyed buildings and got\n**2,813**, **5,048**, **1,641** and **677** —\nover different ground, by different methods, with different definitions of\ndestroyed. None of them is the number.\n\n![The same ground graded twice, two days apart](attachment:fig-reflight.png)\n\nCopernicus graded one area on 29 August, then flew it again on 31 August.\nNothing on the ground changed in between. The second look found\n**653 more buildings\ndestroyed** than the first.\n\nAn early damage count is a floor, not a total. So is the map of where the damage\nis: the volunteer record stops dead at **km 78**, which is where\none mapping project ended and the next began, not where the flood did. The water\nran on for another 130 km past the last red dot on this map.\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%",
          "title": "Four counts of the same thing"
        }
      }
    },
